<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/intermediate/telescopes-and-the-electromagnetic-spectrum">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Astronomy's first instrument rule: <strong>aperture beats everything</strong>. A telescope's
    light-gathering power grows with the <em>area</em> of its mirror — diameter squared — so the
    10-metre Keck telescopes collect a million times more light than your eye. Its second rule:
    visible light is a sliver of the story; the universe broadcasts across the whole
    <strong>electromagnetic spectrum</strong>, and each band needs its own kind of eye.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why size wins — double the mirror, four times the photons; ten times, a hundred" widget="plotter" config={scopeCfg} />

  <h2>The multi-spectrum universe</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Radio (metre–mm):</strong> penetrates dust, reveals pulsars, quasars and the cosmic microwave background — the universe's cold, faint and ancient channels.</li>
    <li><strong>Infrared:</strong> sees through dust clouds where stars are born; essential for distant (redshifted) galaxies. James Webb's 6.5-metre gold-coated mirror is an infrared machine, and it must be cooled to −230 °C so its own warmth doesn't blind it.</li>
    <li><strong>Optical:</strong> the human-familiar band — where large mirrors and adaptive optics (laser-corrected "artificial guide stars") let ground telescopes approach space sharpness.</li>
    <li><strong>X-ray and gamma:</strong> the violent universe — black-hole accretion, supernova remnants. These photons stop in matter, so the telescopes orbit above the atmosphere (Chandra, Fermi).</li>
    <li><strong>Gravitational waves</strong> (2015–) are the newest channel entirely: spacetime itself as the detector. Multi-messenger astronomy — seeing one event in light and in gravity — is the field's newest superpower.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA mission pages (Hubble, JWST, Chandra); LIGO Scientific Collaboration; OpenStax, Astronomy 2e (Telescopes).</p>
</TopicPage>
